+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole number of seconds, like
+`120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups, like `2d` or `1h30m`, using
+the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds).
+
+Each unit may appear at most once, and units must be given in that order. Units
+are case-insensitive, and spaces are not allowed. Negative and decimal values
+are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option(
+        "--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s", show_default=True
+    )
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout}")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
